"CONSUMER CONTRACTS\n(c) Scope.\nThis Restatement restates the common law of contracts as courts have applied it in\nthe context of consumer contracts. It restates the approach courts have taken to determine\nwhich terms are adopted as part of the contract and whether those terms are enforceable.\n(d) Relation to the Restatement of the Law Second, Contracts.\nThe entire Restatement of the Law Second, Contracts, applies to consumer contracts,\nand provides additional rules and principles, including rules of mutual assent, interpretation,\navoidance, and remedies not included in this Restatement. Provisions from the Restatement\nof the Law Second, Contracts, that are particularly suited to the interpretation and\nconstruction of consumer contracts include § 206 on interpretation against the drafter and\n§ 211(3) on protecting the reasonable expectations of the consumer.\n(e) Relation to Statutory Law.\nThis Restatement applies to consumer contracts, except to the extent a matter is\ngoverned by, or application of the rules of this Restatement is displaced by, statute,\nregulation, or other discrete body of law. It restates contract-law principles under state law\nand takes no position on the proper relationship between statutory or regulatory\nrequirements and those principles. In particular, this Restatement neither interprets nor\ndetermines the scope or application of provisions of the Uniform Commercial Code.\n(f) Outline.\nThis Restatement provides specific guidance on the following issues:\n(1) contracts to which this Restatement applies and the definition of this\nRestatement's key terms (§ 1);\n(2) adoption of standard contract terms in a consumer contract to which a\nconsumer manifested assent (§ 2);\n(3) adoption of a modification of the standard contract terms in a consumer\ncontract (§ 3);\n(4) interpretation and construction of consumer contracts (§ 4);\n(5) terms in a consumer contract that grant a business discretion (§ 5);\n(6) terms in a consumer contract that are unenforceable on grounds of\nunconscionability (§ 6);\n12\n@ 1\n"